B.MY=new A.dx("bilingual-medical-lexicon-02","Comprehensive Bengali-English Bedside & Medical AAC Lexicon",B.uh,B.a6a,"Curated 50+ core medical and physiological terms mapped across English and Bengali with semantic equivalence.","Core bedside medical vocabulary mapped for bilingual patient synthesis:\n1. Biological Needs: Water -> \u099c\u09b2/\u09aa\u09be\u09a8\u09bf | Hunger -> \u0995\u09cd\u09b7\u09c1\u09a7\u09be/\u0996\u09be\u09ac\u09be\u09b0 | Sleep -> \u0998\u09c1\u09ae/\u09ac\u09bf\u09b6\u09cd\u09b0\u09be\u09ae | Bathroom -> \u09aa\u09cd\u09b0\u09b8\u09cd\u09b0\u09be\u09ac/\u099f\u09af\u09bc\u09b2\u09c7\u099f\n2. Acute Discomfort: Severe Pain -> \u09a4\u09c0\u09ac\u09cd\u09b0 \u09ac\u09cd\u09af\u09a5\u09be | Difficulty Breathing -> \u09b6\u09cd\u09ac\u09be\u09b8\u0995\u09b7\u09cd\u099f/\u09a6\u09ae \u0986\u099f\u0995\u09c7 \u0986\u09b8\u099b\u09c7 | Cold -> \u09a0\u09be\u09a8\u09cd\u09a1\u09be \u09b2\u09be\u0997\u099b\u09c7 | Hot -> \u0997\u09b0\u09ae \u09b2\u09be\u0997\u099b\u09c7\n3. Physical Positioning: Sit Up -> \u09b8\u09cb\u099c\u09be \u0995\u09b0\u09c7 \u09ac\u09b8\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09c7 \u09a6\u09bf\u09a8 | Lie Down -> \u09b6\u09c1\u0987\u09af\u09bc\u09c7 \u09a6\u09bf\u09a8 | Turn Left/Right -> \u09aa\u09be\u09b6 \u09ab\u09bf\u09b0\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09c7 \u09a6\u09bf\u09a8 | Fix Pillow -> \u09ac\u09be\u09b2\u09bf\u09b6 \u09a0\u09bf\u0995 \u0995\u09b0\u09c1\u09a8\n4. Personnel & Care: Call Doctor -> \u09a1\u09be\u0995\u09cd\u09a4\u09be\u09b0 \u09a1\u09be\u0995\u09c1\u09a8 | Call Nurse -> \u09a8\u09be\u09b0\u09cd\u09b8 \u09a1\u09be\u0995\u09c1\u09a8 | Family -> \u09aa\u09b0\u09bf\u09ac\u09be\u09b0\u09c7\u09b0 \u09b8\u09a6\u09b8\u09cd\u09af | Medication -> \u09b8\u09ae\u09af\u09bc\u09ae\u09a4\u09cb \u0993\u09b7\u09c1\u09a7 \u09a6\u09bf\u09a8")
B.ML=new A.et(15,"strokeRehabilitation")
B.a7b=s(["stroke rehabilitation","hemiparesis","motor relearning","neuroplasticity","rehab","exercise","arm recovery","paretic limb","constraint","repetition"],t.s)
B.N1=new A.dx("stroke-rehab-01","Stroke Rehabilitation & Neuroplastic Motor Relearning",B.ML,B.a7b,"Clinical guidelines for stroke motor rehabilitation, neuroplasticity pacing, and bilateral limb guidance.","Stroke Motor Recovery & Rehabilitation Principles:\n1. Repetitive Task-Oriented Training: Neuroplastic cortical reorganization occurs through focused, purposeful repetitions. Encourage 10 to 15 deliberate reaching or finger flexion movements per session rather than fatigue-inducing bursts.\n2. Bilateral Arm Training: Mirroring healthy hand movements with the paretic hand stimulates homologous motor cortex networks.\n3. Gradual Pacing: Stop immediately if joint pain occurs. Compensatory shoulder hiking should be discouraged in favor of neutral alignment.\n4. Asha Coaching Loop: Asha initiates gentle prompts: Let us move your neck slowly. Turn right... Good. Now slightly more... Excellent.")
B.MM=new A.et(16,"speechTherapy")
B.a5r=s(["speech therapy","dysarthria","aphasia","articulation","phoneme","pacing","vocal fatigue","speech","voice","swallowing"],t.s)
B.Nl=new A.dx("speech-therapy-01","Speech Therapy, Dysarthria Pacing & Articulation Exercises",B.MM,B.a5r,"Evidence-based speech therapy strategies, oral-motor drills, and dysarthria compensatory pacing.","Clinical Speech-Language Pathology Guidelines:\n1. Pacing & Rate Reduction: In flaccid or spastic dysarthria, slowing syllable production improves consonant intelligibility by 40%.\n2. Phoneme Shaping Drills: Focus on bilabial (/p/, /b/, /m/) and alveolar (/t/, /d/, /n/) sounds with high visual contrast.\n3. Vocal Fatigue Management: Schedule high-demand conversational exercises after morning rest periods. Provide instant AAC text when vocal cord fatigue sets in.\n4. Respiratory-Phonatory Coordination: Instruct patient to inhale comfortably through diaphragm before initiating short 2-3 word utterances.")
B.MN=new A.et(17,"autismSupport")
B.a9e=s(["autism","asd","sensory overload","regulation","visual schedule","low cognitive load","stimming","meltdown","aac"],t.s)
B.Nc=new A.dx("autism-support-01","Autism Spectrum Support, Sensory Regulation & Low-Cognitive AAC",B.MN,B.a9e,"Sensory regulation protocols, predictable visual AAC scheduling, and de-escalation for neurodivergent users.","Autism Assistive Support Protocol:\n1. Sensory Predictability: Minimize sudden audio or visual animations. Use consistent muted colors and predictable screen layouts.\n2. Visual Scheduling: Provide chronological step-by-step cue cards (First water, then rest, then exercise) to reduce transition anxiety.\n3. Overload De-escalation: If rapid involuntary movements or distress signals are detected, reduce companion audio volume, dim lighting, and present calming breathing prompts.\n4. Communication Respect: Recognize non-speaking does not mean non-understanding. Keep tone respectful, calm, and direct.")
B.MO=new A.et(18,"icuCommunication")
B.a6b=s(["icu","intensive care","intubation","endotracheal","ventilator","pain scale","eye blink","critical care","non-verbal"],t.s)
B.N8=new A.dx("icu-comm-01","ICU Communication Boards & Acute Intubation AAC Protocols",B.MO,B.a6b,"Fast-path communication boards, binary eye-blink confirmation, and acute pain scales for intubated patients.","Intensive Care Unit (ICU) Communication Standard:\n1. Binary Eye-Blink Protocol: Two blinks = YES, prolonged eye closure (1.5s) = NO. Allows intubated patients to verify biological needs without vocal cords.\n2. Priority Biological Grid: Immediate 1-tap access to Pain, Suction airway, Reposition bed, Cold/Hot, Family member.\n3. PAINAD / CPOT Non-Verbal Scoring: Asha observes facial muscle furrowing and brow tension to estimate pain score (0 to 10) even when patient cannot speak.\n4. Nurse Call Integration: Urgent ICU requests trigger immediate dual alerts on both bedside display and nurse station webhook.")
B.MP=new A.et(19,"physiotherapy")
B.a7k=s(["physiotherapy","physical therapy","range of motion","spasticity","contracture","neck movement","hand stretch","joint mobility"],t.s)
B.N_=new A.dx("physiotherapy-01","Neurological Physiotherapy, Range of Motion & Spasticity Management",B.MP,B.a7k,"Bedside and wheelchair range of motion exercises, contracture prevention, and gentle stretching protocols.","Assistive Physiotherapy & Movement Guidelines:\n1. Cervical & Neck Range of Motion: Gentle lateral rotation (ear toward shoulder) held for 5 seconds improves carotid circulation and reduces cervical tension.\n2. Passive Wrist & Hand Stretching: Slowly extend fingers using opposite hand or armrest wedge to counteract flexor spasticity common in hemiplegia.\n3. Postural Alignment: Verify wheelchair pelvis position is seated deep against backrest. Avoid sacral sitting which exacerbates spinal deformities.\n4. Asha Feedback Loop: Track execution smoothness. Provide positive reinforcement: Good, you completed 5 gentle repetitions today.")
B.MR=new A.et(20,"caregiverGuidelines")
